# `-d s3` only — the S3-backed LIBRARY on the Unraid box.
# Read as [.kamal/secrets-common, .kamal/secrets.s3]; .kamal/secrets (base) is NOT read.
# Safe for git: references and commands only, never a raw credential.

# The s3 LIBRARY reuses the chunk store's bucket and identity (my-bucket / seaweednew) under a
# disjoint `library/` prefix — no new bucket and no new credential, just a second variable name.
# So this is the same 1Password field as base's CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, deliberately named for
# the variable the s3 overlay's env.secret actually declares.
#
# This replaces the old `LIBRARY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` indirection in
# secrets-common, which only worked because you had exported CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY by hand
# first. Reading 1Password directly removes that prerequisite. If you still want to override,
# export LIBRARY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (the variable this leg actually uses) — exporting
# CHUNK_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY no longer reaches this leg, because the s3 container never sets it.
LIBRARY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=$(bin/secret LIBRARY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY 'op://Personal/seaweednew/secret key')
